WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:05.030
It's hard to believe it's been more than 30
years since James Cameron made The Terminator,

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:08.390
but several movies down the line, the franchise
has lost its steam.

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:13.090
Cameron told an amazing story across Terminator
and Terminator II: Judgment Day, but not all

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:15.820
franchises are meant to run for three decades.

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:19.020
Here's a look at why Hollywood won't make
another Terminator movie.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.680
Defective hardware

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:25.060
Let's be real: The past few sequels were pretty
much total train wrecks.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:29.650
Things started to go downhill with 2003's
Terminator 3: Rise of the Machines, the first

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:30.790
without Cameron at the helm.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.240
“Talk to the hand.”

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:40.660
The decline continued with 2009's Terminator:
Salvation, which despite the presence of splashy

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:46.220
action director McG, somehow managed to make
the future battle with Skynet utterly boring.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:47.860
“Who are you?”

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:49.840
“John Connor!”

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:55.210
That necessitated the full-on reboot in 2015's
Terminator: Genisys, which was met with such

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:59.970
critical disdain and box office indifference
that planned sequels were cancelled.

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:01.100
Yuck.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.430
Cursed without Cameron

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:06.290
The first two films were awesome for one obvious
reason.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:09.050
And no, it wasn't naked Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:11.190
“Your clothes.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:12.190
Give them to me.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:13.190
Now.”

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:16.920
So when writer-director James Cameron decided
to leave the franchise after Terminator II:

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:21.580
Judgment Day, Hollywood should have probably
just left well enough alone and ended the

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:22.930
series right there.

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:26.660
It's telling that Cameron says he's not a
"big fan" of the sequels that followed, as

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:29.630
he doesn't feel they "lived up to that potential."

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:32.700
And without Cameron, they probably never will.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.270
Disenfranchised

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:38.010
One big issue with the series is that it wasn't
designed to be a series at all.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:43.420
Both the original film and Terminator II weren't
really about time travel, but instead focused

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.470
on how knowledge of the impending Armageddon
affected the characters.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:51.750
Sarah Connor found her inner strength, the
Terminator found his humanity, John discovered

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:52.750
his destiny.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:56.920
No amount of modern special effects can disguise
the fact that the sequels are missing the

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.760
heart of the original films.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:04.540
Instead, they've relied on increasingly bloated,
confusing, and convoluted plots, trading humanity

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:06.820
for incomprehensible spectacle.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:10.580
Without the original films’ focus on what
it means to be human, the sequels are destined

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:14.060
to be pale imitations rather than worthy successors.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:15.350
Arn-old

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:20.120
Other than James Cameron, the major driving
force behind the franchise is Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:24.700
You have to give him credit for suiting up
yet again in Terminator: Genisys despite the

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.440
fact that he was pushing 70.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:30.540
But even he has his limits, and the time is
fast approaching when he'll no longer be able

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.410
to pull off a Terminator film.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:35.260
And would fans really want to see more sequels
without him?

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.580
Given how little moviegoers have wanted to
see the last bunch even with him present in

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:43.700
the cast, making more sequels without Arnold
seems like a losing formula.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.000
“I’ll be back.”

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:48.990
Mmm...this time, you probably won't.

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.830
Terminator for teens

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:55.310
The first two Terminator films wore their
R ratings like a badge of honor, catering

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:59.420
to adults with dark stories not afraid to
feature some violence and language.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:04.590
But the last two films diluted the series,
softening enough to earn a PG-13 rating.

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:09.550
That allowed a larger potential audience,
sure, but it also alienated long time fans

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:13.370
by smoothing the title's hard edges and dumbing
down the action.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:16.020
Pandering is never going to result in better
movies.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:17.820
“Oh, oh great.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:18.820
That’s just great.”

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:21.740
“I did not kill him.”

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.390
Factory reset

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:28.260
It turns out if you keep churning out bad
movies, the public will eventually stop turning

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.370
out to see them.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:30.370
Who knew?

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:34.670
The past few Terminator films have failed
to generate much box office buzz, despite

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:37.580
ballooning budgets and massive marketing campaigns.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:42.569
Terminator 3 pulled in $150 million in the
U.S., and it only went downhill from there.

00:03:42.569 --> 00:03:48.120
Terminator: Salvation made $125 million in
the U.S., and the latest sequel, Terminator:

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.920
Genisys, scored a mere $89 million.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:51.920
Ouch.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:56.050
Hollywood leans on known franchises because
they're more bankable brands than unknown

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:59.940
commodities, but sometimes even the name brands
run out of steam.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:04.970
In the wake of Genisys' failure, the smart
money is sadly no longer on Terminator.

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:06.680
No fate but what we make

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:10.020
Though the situation looks dire now, there's
still hope.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.520
“Come with me if you want to live.”

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:13.520
The reason?

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:17.099
The rights to The Terminator revert back to
Cameron in 2019.

00:04:17.099 --> 00:04:22.360
While he's currently busy with those long-promised
Avatar sequels, it's possible he might eventually

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:24.479
make another Terminator film himself.

00:04:24.479 --> 00:04:29.639
And if not, you can bet that he'll be very
careful just who he licenses the rights to.

00:04:29.639 --> 00:04:34.020
With Cameron regaining creative control over
the franchise, one thing is sure: if there

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:38.430
are never any more Terminator movies, that
also means there won't be any more bad Terminator

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:39.430
movies.

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:40.479
We'll count that as a win.

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:41.710
Thanks for watching!

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:44.620
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:46.990
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

